WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.620 --> 00:00:05.609
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:05.609 --> 00:00:11.410
Today, we're going to crush some soda cans.

00:00:11.410 --> 00:00:16.330
In the footage that you see here, the animations
are performed by an already existing algorithm

00:00:16.330 --> 00:00:22.920
for thin shell deformations, and for a complete
sensorial experience, this piece of work aims

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:25.800
synthesize sound for these phenomena.

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:30.380
Sounds for crumpling up all kinds of candy
wraps, foils, and plastic bags.

00:00:30.380 --> 00:00:33.679
A lofty, noble goal, loving it.

00:00:33.679 --> 00:00:36.629
However, this problem is extraordinarily difficult.

00:00:36.629 --> 00:00:41.699
The reason is that these crumpling simulations
are amazingly detailed, and even if we knew

00:00:41.699 --> 00:00:46.950
all the physical laws for the sound synthesis,
which is already pretty crazy, it would still

00:00:46.950 --> 00:00:52.539
be a fruitless endeavor to take into consideration
every single thing that takes place in the

00:00:52.539 --> 00:00:53.539
simulation.

00:00:53.539 --> 00:00:58.199
We have to come up with ways to cut corners
to decrease the execution time of our algorithm.

00:00:58.199 --> 00:01:03.500
Running a naive, exhaustive search would take
tens of hours for only several seconds of

00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:04.500
footage.

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:07.830
And the big question is, of course, what can
we do about it?

00:01:07.830 --> 00:01:12.660
And before we proceed, just a quick reminder
that the geometry of these models are given

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:17.420
by a lot of connected points that people in
computer graphics like to call vertices.

00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:23.250
The sound synthesis takes place by observing
the changes in the stiffness of these models,

00:01:23.250 --> 00:01:25.300
which is the source of the crumpling noise.

00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:30.240
Normally, our sound simulation scales with
the number of vertices, and it is abundantly

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:34.360
clear that there are simply too many of them
to go through one by one.

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:38.520
To this end, we should strive to reduce the
complexity of this problem.

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:43.630
First, we start with identifying and discarding
the less significant vibration modes.

00:01:43.630 --> 00:01:49.290
Beyond that if in one of these vertices, we
observe that a similar kind of buckling behavior

00:01:49.290 --> 00:01:54.810
is present in its neighborhood, we group up
these vertices into a patch, and we then forget

00:01:54.810 --> 00:01:59.340
about the vertices and run the sound synthesis
on these patches.

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:04.310
And of course, the number of patches is significantly
less than the number of vertices in the original

00:02:04.310 --> 00:02:05.310
model.

00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:07.220
In this footage, you can see some of these
patches.

00:02:07.220 --> 00:02:13.500
And it turns out that the execution time can
be significantly decreased by these optimizations.

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:18.160
With these techniques, we can expect results
in at least 5 times quicker, but if we're

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:23.430
willing to introduce slight degradations to
the quality of the sounds, we can even go

00:02:23.430 --> 00:02:26.290
10 times quicker with barely perceptible changes.

00:02:26.290 --> 00:02:31.639
To evaluate the quality of the solutions,
there is a user study presented in the paper.

00:02:31.639 --> 00:02:36.750
And the pinnacle of all tests is of course,
when we let reality be our judge.

00:02:36.750 --> 00:02:41.719
Everything so far sounds great on paper, but
how does it compare to what we experience

00:02:41.719 --> 00:02:42.759
in reality?

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:16.240
Wow.

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:18.190
Truly excellent results.

00:03:18.190 --> 00:03:21.560
Suffice to say, they are absolutely crushing
it.

00:03:21.560 --> 00:03:26.079
And we haven't even talked about stochastic
enrichment and how one of these problems can

00:03:26.079 --> 00:03:29.020
be solved optimally via dynamic programming.

00:03:29.020 --> 00:03:31.980
If you're interested, make sure to have a
look at the paper!

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:35.700
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

